Let a thunk that was interrupted be forced again.

A backport of NixOS/nix 5c4f498d3, "Don't memoise Interrupted errors"
(NixOS/nix#15980), first released in 2.35.0, so only the 2.34 lane
takes it. huggorm carries the same file, and nanopynix#309 measured it
here.

EvalState::handleEvalExceptionForThunk and handleEvalExceptionForApp store
the exception of a failed evaluation in the value, with mkFailed. They keep a
recovery thunk only for RecoverableEvalError. nix::Interrupted derives
BaseError, so 2.34 caches an interruption like an evaluation error, and every
later force of that value rethrows it. `nix` never sees this, because an
interrupt ends the process. An embedder that keeps an EvalState after a
cancelled call does, measured on 2.34.8 with
`{ a = builtins.toJSON (builtins.genList (x: x) 12000000); }`:

    force a under an interrupt, cancelled at 0.2s  -> Interrupted, 0.34s
    force a again, not interrupted                 -> Interrupted, 0.00s

The change is upstream's, line for line. The diff is not: it is taken after
nix-base-env-size.patch, with six lines of context, so it applies last.
The two handlers share their first lines, so upstream's three-line hunks
matched the wrong handler once the earlier patch moved the line numbers.

